{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgnZc94PuawT5bGmHRakH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I'm checking the control results: the overlap of late-layer input sides with Bf turns out to be an artifact of D_f weighting, since the control gives the same value, so that earlier claim about reading predictions in D_f coordinates doesn't hold. Next I'm looking at the OV copy changes, which still stand against the control for L15 through L17.\n\nI'm moving on to the QK matching results, which also don't survive the control: L15H1 drops to −1.69 rather than −13, and L14H0 is only marginal, so the identity-match findings were mostly a D_f weighting artifact and I'm retracting the L15H1 claim. That leaves the OV copy results in L15/L16 and L17 as the robust evidence, and I'm now questioning whether the early-layer e→e copying needs its own control, since B may be concentrated on dimensions with small D_pf·D_f.\n\nI'm now going to build a control that matches B's per-dimension energy profile, so random subspaces can't pick up the same dimension-weighting artifact. That would apply to the early e→e copying, the late f→readout tests, and the MLP capacity checks. I'm also weighing whether the input side should stay in e coordinates via B, since runs 15 and 16 already showed a similar but weaker pattern that way.\n\nI'm now considering whether the MLP capacity checks need the same energy-matched control, since their subspaces could pick up the same dimension-weighting effect.\n\n","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7784,"cache_read_input_tokens":201591,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":7784},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null}